\DOC LOCAL_JADD_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_JADD_TAC : int -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances an {eventually}-style machine-state goal across a called
point-addition subroutine, replacing the pre-call state assumptions by
post-call ones together with a specification of the subroutine's result.

\DESCRIBE
The tactic takes one integer argument {n+1}, the index of the new state
variable; the resulting goal and all its new assumptions are phrased in terms
of that numbered state, i.e. the previous state's index plus one.
{
   A ?- eventually <machine> P s<n>
   ================================================  LOCAL_JADD_TAC (n+1)
   A' ?- eventually <machine> P s<n+1>
}
\noindent Here {A'} is obtained from {A} as follows. The assumptions about the
pre-call state — the register and memory reads, the loaded-program fact, and
the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} frame relating the initial state to {s<n>} — are
discharged, and corresponding assumptions about the new state are added: the
same memory/register contents re-expressed at {s<n+1>}, program-counter and
stack-pointer readings reflecting the return from the call, a {MAYCHANGE} fact
extended with the regions written by the call, and one new assumption of the
form {!z1 ... zp. <hypotheses on the inputs> ==> <memory triple at the
subroutine's output address represents the group sum of the two input points>}.
Assumptions not mentioning a state (arithmetic side conditions, non-overlap
facts, previously derived specifications) are left untouched.

\COMMENTS
All observed traces show this single kind of transformation, on goals whose
conclusion is an {eventually} statement about a concrete state variable.

\ENDDOC
